  "eyebrow": "SAT Math / 03",
  "title": "Exponents, radicals, and complex numbers",
  "subtitle": "Simplify powers and roots, model exponential change, solve radical equations, and work confidently with i.",
  "materi": [
    {
      "judul": "Exponent rules",
      "icon": "fa-superscript",
      "tipe": "table",
      "isi": [
        "An exponent tells how many times a base is used as a factor. Before combining exponents, make sure the bases are the same.",
        {
          "type": "table",
          "table": {
            "headers": [
              "Rule",
              "Equivalent form",
              "Example"
            ],
            "leftAlign": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Product of powers",
                "\\(a^m\\cdot a^n=a^{m+n}\\)",
                "\\(x^3x^8=x^{11}\\)"
              ],
              [
                "Quotient of powers",
                "\\(\\dfrac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}\\)",
                "\\(\\dfrac{x^4}{x^9}=x^{-5}=\\dfrac1{x^5}\\)"
              ],
              [
                "Power of a power",
                "\\((a^m)^n=a^{mn}\\)",
                "\\((x^4)^3=x^{12}\\)"
              ],
              [
                "Zero exponent",
                "\\(a^0=1\\), for \\(a\\ne0\\)",
                "\\(17^0=1\\)"
              ],
              [
                "Negative exponent",
                "\\(a^{-n}=\\dfrac1{a^n}\\)",
                "\\(x^{-2}=\\dfrac1{x^2}\\)"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "content": "Do not add exponents across addition: \\(x^2+x^3\\) is not \\(x^5\\). Exponent rules apply to multiplication, division, and powers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "judul": "Rational exponents and radicals",
      "icon": "fa-root",
      "tipe": "basic",
      "isi": [
        "A fractional exponent is another notation for a root. The denominator identifies the root; the numerator identifies the power.",
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "content": "\\[a^{1/n}=\\sqrt[n]{a},\\qquad a^{m/n}=\\sqrt[n]{a^m}=\\left(\\sqrt[n]{a}\\right)^m\\]"
        },
        {
          "type": "grid",
          "items": [
            {
              "title": "\\(x^{1/2}\\)",
              "content": "Square root: \\(\\sqrt{x}\\)"
            },
            {
              "title": "\\(x^{1/3}\\)",
              "content": "Cube root: \\(\\sqrt[3]{x}\\)"
            },
            {
              "title": "\\(x^{2/3}\\)",
              "content": "Cube root, then square: \\(\\sqrt[3]{x^2}\\)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Example: rewrite before simplifying",
          "content": "\\(\\sqrt[3]{x^{5/6}}=(x^{5/6})^{1/3}=x^{5/18}\\). Multiplying the exponents is usually the fastest route."
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "content": "For even roots in the real numbers, the radicand must be nonnegative. Odd roots can accept negative radicands."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "judul": "Simplifying radicals and absolute value",
      "icon": "fa-square-root-variable",
      "tipe": "basic",
      "isi": [
        "To simplify a square root, factor out the largest perfect square. Only factors that occur in pairs can leave the radical.",
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Example: extract a perfect square",
          "content": "\\(\\sqrt{72}=\\sqrt{36\\cdot2}=6\\sqrt2\\). Likewise, \\(\\sqrt{108}=\\sqrt{36\\cdot3}=6\\sqrt3\\)."
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "content": "\\[\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|\\qquad\\text{and, if }x>0,\\qquad\\sqrt{9x^2}=3x\\]"
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "content": "The principal square root is never negative. Therefore \\(\\sqrt{(-4)^2}=4\\), not \\(-4\\). Use \\(|x|\\) unless the problem gives a condition such as \\(x>0\\)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "judul": "Exponential models",
      "icon": "fa-chart-line",
      "tipe": "table",
      "isi": [
        "Exponential models describe repeated multiplication. The general form is \\(A(t)=A_0b^t\\), where \\(A_0\\) is the initial amount and \\(b\\) is the multiplier for one time unit.",
        {
          "type": "table",
          "table": {
            "headers": [
              "Situation",
              "Model",
              "Multiplier"
            ],
            "leftAlign": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Doubles every \\(k\\) time units",
                "\\(A(t)=A_0\\cdot2^{t/k}\\)",
                "\\(2\\)"
              ],
              [
                "Grows by \\(r\\)% each period",
                "\\(A(t)=A_0\\left(1+\\dfrac r{100}\\right)^t\\)",
                "\\(1+\\dfrac r{100}\\)"
              ],
              [
                "Decreases by \\(r\\)% each period",
                "\\(A(t)=A_0\\left(1-\\dfrac r{100}\\right)^t\\)",
                "\\(1-\\dfrac r{100}\\)"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Example: doubling",
          "content": "A town has population \\(8{,}000\\) and doubles every 5 years. After 15 years, there are \\(15\\div5=3\\) doubling periods, so the model is \\(8000\\cdot2^3\\)."
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "content": "Count periods, not just the number of years. A 3% annual increase uses multiplier \\(1.03\\), not \\(0.03\\)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "judul": "Solving radical equations",
      "icon": "fa-equals",
      "tipe": "basic",
      "isi": [
        "Isolate the radical first. Then raise both sides to the appropriate power and check every candidate in the original equation.",
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Example: a linear radical equation",
          "content": "Solve \\(7\\sqrt{x}-24=11\\). Add 24: \\(7\\sqrt{x}=35\\). Divide by 7: \\(\\sqrt{x}=5\\). Square both sides: \\(x=25\\)."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Example: check for an extraneous solution",
          "content": "Solve \\(\\sqrt{2x+3}=x-1\\). Since the left side is nonnegative, require \\(x\\ge1\\). Squaring gives \\(2x+3=(x-1)^2\\), or \\(x^2-4x-2=0\\). The candidates are \\(2\\pm\\sqrt6\\); only \\(2+\\sqrt6\\) satisfies \\(x\\ge1\\) and the original equation."
        },
        {
          "type": "ordered-list",
          "items": [
            "State any domain restriction, especially when an even root is involved.",
            "Isolate the radical.",
            "Raise both sides to a power.",
            "Substitute each candidate into the original equation."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "judul": "Complex numbers and powers of i",
      "icon": "fa-i-cursor",
      "tipe": "table",
      "isi": [
        "The imaginary unit is defined by \\(i=\\sqrt{-1}\\), so \\(i^2=-1\\). A complex number is written as \\(a+bi\\), with real part \\(a\\) and imaginary coefficient \\(b\\).",
        {
          "type": "table",
          "table": {
            "headers": [
              "Power",
              "Value",
              "Cycle position"
            ],
            "leftAlign": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "\\(i^1\\)",
                "\\(i\\)",
                "remainder 1"
              ],
              [
                "\\(i^2\\)",
                "\\(-1\\)",
                "remainder 2"
              ],
              [
                "\\(i^3\\)",
                "\\(-i\\)",
                "remainder 3"
              ],
              [
                "\\(i^4\\)",
                "\\(1\\)",
                "remainder 0"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Example: simplify a product",
          "content": "\\((3+i)(2-i)=6-3i+2i-i^2=6-i+1=7-i\\). Expand first, then replace \\(i^2\\) with \\(-1\\)."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Example: reduce a large power",
          "content": "Because powers of \\(i\\) repeat every 4, \\(i^{23}=i^{4\\cdot5+3}=i^3=-i\\)."
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "content": "Add and subtract complex numbers by combining real parts with real parts and imaginary terms with imaginary terms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "judul": "SAT checklist for this topic",
      "icon": "fa-list-check",
      "tipe": "basic",
      "isi": [
        "Use the form that makes the structure easiest to see: exponential, radical, factored, or complex-number form.",
        {
          "type": "grid",
          "items": [
            {
              "title": "Same base?",
              "content": "Only combine exponents when multiplication or division involves the same base."
            },
            {
              "title": "Domain?",
              "content": "Check nonnegative radicands, nonzero denominators, and conditions such as \\(x>0\\)."
            },
            {
              "title": "Extraneous?",
              "content": "After squaring a radical equation, verify in the original equation."
            },
            {
              "title": "Complex form?",
              "content": "Replace \\(i^2\\) with \\(-1\\) and reduce powers of \\(i\\) modulo 4."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
